---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import InstallCommand from "../../components/InstallCommand.astro";
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";

const tocEntries = [
  { id: "what-plugdash-is", text: "What plugdash is", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "how-emdash-plugins-work", text: "How EmDash plugins work", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "install-readtime", text: "Install readtime", level: 2 as const, children: [
    { id: "step-1-install", text: "Step 1: install", level: 3 as const, children: [] },
    { id: "step-2-add-the-component", text: "Step 2: add the component", level: 3 as const, children: [] },
    { id: "step-3-register-plugins-with-hooks", text: "Step 3: register plugins with hooks", level: 3 as const, children: [] },
  ] },
  { id: "change-the-look", text: "Change the look", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
  { id: "next-steps", text: "Next steps", level: 2 as const, children: [] },
];
---

<Doc
  tocEntries={tocEntries}
  currentSlug="getting-started"
  title="Getting started"
  section="on-ramp"
  description="Install your first plugdash plugin in five minutes."
>
  <h2 id="what-plugdash-is">What PlugDash is</h2>
  <p>
    PlugDash is a plugin catalog for EmDash. Every plugin ships as an npm
    package, a companion Astro component, and a short list of CSS tokens
    you can override. Install one, import one component, and your readers
    see the result.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site runs on EmDash with the plugins a server-rendered blog
    needs. The share buttons on this page are sharepost. The demo post
    on the home page shows reading time, table of contents, hearts,
    short links and share buttons on a real post.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-emdash-plugins-work">How EmDash plugins work</h2>
  <p>
    PlugDash plugins come in two kinds. Render-time plugins (readtime,
    tocgen, sharepost) are only a component: it reads the post when the
    page renders and stores nothing. Plugins with hooks run inside
    EmDash when content changes, usually on publish, and store their
    result somewhere the component can read it: plugin KV for heart
    counts, native redirects for short links, the post's SEO image for
    social cards, the code block itself for highlighted code.
  </p>
  <p>
    Plugins never write to a shared <code>metadata</code> field. The
    official EmDash templates do not have one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install-readtime">Install readtime</h2>
  <p>
    readtime is the simplest plugin in the catalog. Its component counts
    the words in the post when the page renders. Nothing is stored and
    nothing needs registering. Use it as your worked example.
  </p>

  <h3 id="step-1-install">Step 1: install</h3>
  <InstallCommand command="npm install @plugdash/readtime" />

  <h3 id="step-2-add-the-component">Step 2: add the component</h3>
  <p>In your post layout:</p>
  <Code language="astro" code={`---
import ReadingTime from "@plugdash/readtime/ReadingTime.astro"
---
<ReadingTime post={post} />`} />
  <p>
    Reload a post. The reading time appears. There is no
    <code>astro.config.mjs</code> change for readtime, tocgen or
    sharepost.
  </p>

  <h3 id="step-3-register-plugins-with-hooks">Step 3: register plugins with hooks</h3>
  <p>
    Plugins that store data or react to publishing (heartpost, shortlink,
    codeblock, socialcard, enrichkit, autobuild, callout) are registered
    in the <code>plugins</code> array of the <code>emdash()</code>
    integration:
  </p>
  <Code language="js" filename="astro.config.mjs" code={`import { defineConfig } from "astro/config"
import emdash from "emdash/astro"
import { heartpostPlugin } from "@plugdash/heartpost"

export default defineConfig({
  integrations: [
    emdash({
      plugins: [
        heartpostPlugin({ collections: ["posts"] }),
      ],
    }),
  ],
})`} />
  <p>
    Each plugin doc has the exact snippet and options.
  </p>

  <h2 id="change-the-look">Change the look</h2>
  <p>
    Every PlugDash component reads from CSS custom properties under the
    <code>--plugdash-*</code> namespace. To restyle, override them in your
    global stylesheet:
  </p>
  <Code language="css" code={`:root {
  --plugdash-accent: #6366f1;
  --plugdash-muted: #888;
  --plugdash-rt-radius: 4px;
}`} />
  <p>
    If you need more than tokens can give you, copy the component's
    <code>.astro</code> file out of <code>node_modules/@plugdash/*/src/</code>
    into your theme and modify it freely. Components are intentionally
    simple and have no upstream coupling.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
  <p>
    Each plugin has its own doc with install, props, CSS tokens, and a
    live demo of its companion component:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/docs/readtime">readtime</a> - reading time estimates</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/tocgen">tocgen</a> - nested table of contents</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/callout">callout</a> - info, warning, tip, danger blocks</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/sharepost">sharepost</a> - share buttons with no JS libraries</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/heartpost">heartpost</a> - KV-backed heart counter</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/shortlink">shortlink</a> - short URLs per post</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/engage">engage</a> - heart + share + copy bundle</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/autobuild">autobuild</a> - publish-triggered rebuilds for static sites</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/codeblock">codeblock</a> - syntax highlighting done at save time</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/enrichkit">enrichkit</a> - AI summaries, tags and tweet drafts on publish, stored in plugin KV</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/socialcard">socialcard</a> - OG share PNGs on publish, set as the post's SEO image</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/fromghost">fromghost</a> - import a Ghost export with the @plugdash/import CLI</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/fromsubstack">fromsubstack</a> - import a Substack export with the @plugdash/import CLI</li>
  </ul>
</Doc>
